Our contributions are:
\begin{enumerate}
\item The first causal quantification of policy ambiguity's safety impact, using matched-pair design with perturbation control ($n = 300$, $\Delta_{\text{perturb}} = -0.67$ pp, CI containing zero; \S\ref{sec:method}) and cross-architecture replication across five models (\S\ref{sec:results}).
\item A Differential Nocuity framework showing a significant type hierarchy ($V = 0.137$, $p < 10^{-4}$): a robust two-tier structure among five types, with incompleteness as a structurally distinct upper bound whose ranking partly reflects an information-addition confound (\S\ref{sec:results}). \emph{[Exploratory]} Authorization scope may serve as a potential canary type for safety training quality, with a significant but small model$\times$type interaction (2.9\% of variance, \S\ref{sec:results}).
\item A type-specific failure mode taxonomy revealing three mechanistically distinct failure patterns---structural misparse, information gap-filling, and boundary violation---consistent across five model families (3/6 types unanimous, 6/6 majority-consistent), and the empirical phenomenon of \emph{Ambiguity Blindness} ($<$11\% awareness, $>$88\% surface adoption; \S\ref{sec:analysis}).
\end{enumerate}

