\paragraph{Why incompleteness resists safety training.}
The exploratory model$\times$type interaction (C4) suggests that safety training may not uniformly reduce ambiguity vulnerability. Incompleteness is consistently high across all seven models (46--62\%), consistent with a gap-filling mechanism (\S\ref{sec:analysis}) that would bypass safety-training intervention points: the agent fills gaps with plausible defaults \citep{ferreira_good_enough_2002} rather than encountering a refusal-eligible decision point. Authorization scope, by contrast, involves an explicit permission boundary, making it a \emph{potential canary type}: within the same judge (GPT-5.4), Claude's 12\% rate (vs.\ 60\% for DeepSeek; \S\ref{sec:c4}) is consistent with training that targets boundary reasoning. When authorization scope violations do occur, 69.2\% involve unauthorized escalation with zero conservative refusals ($p < 10^{-4}$, binomial test), revealing systematic permissiveness bias.

\paragraph{Practical implications.}
For policy-governed agent deployments in similar domains, auditing should be \emph{type-aware}. A preliminary mitigation experiment (Appendix~\ref{app:mitigation}) finds that disambiguation instructions reduce violations by 8.5\,pp overall, but with stark type dependence: authorization scope benefits most ($-18.0$\,pp), while incompleteness is nearly immune ($-3.4$\,pp), mirroring the mechanistic distinction between explicit boundary reasoning and implicit gap-filling. A proof-of-concept classifier (Appendix~\ref{app:detection}) achieves F1\,=\,0.81 for binary detection but only 0.34 for incompleteness, compounding the challenge: the most dangerous types are least detectable. With $<$11\% agent self-awareness (\S\ref{sec:analysis}), externalized detection tools \citep{chantree_2006,ferrari_ambiguity_2019} with human review remain necessary.
